Recap, replacing my earlier notes.

Correctness looks good: suite green, and fuzzing 400 random trees x 14 frames against 8.2 gives byte-identical renderer output and identical screen state. Byte volume and write count to the terminal are unchanged too.

Performance, real terminal at 202x61:

| case | 8.2 | PR |
| --- | --- | --- |
| deep tree, 16 levels of layout containers | 90 fps | 155 fps |
| full screen, every row changes | 122 fps | 120 fps |
| content fitting the viewport | 2067 fps | 1873 fps |
| sidebar 200 \| main 300, one row changes | 147 fps | 30 fps |

The last row is the one to look at, and it is the scenario from the description: a container holding far more children than the terminal has rows, with almost nothing changing. `ConcatenatedLineBuffer::getLine()` walks the segment list on each call, and `layoutHorizontal()` calls it once per row per child while rebuilding every row, so the reuse is discarded and replaced by an O(rows x segments) walk. At 976 children per column with a single line changed, render goes from 5.95ms to 83.88ms.

Recording each segment's start offset and count in the constructor, binary searching on a miss, and remembering the last resolved segment brings that case back to the same order as 8.2 and leaves the other three untouched, tests still green.

Two smaller ones: `findCursorPosition()` now returns the last cursor marker where `prepareLines()` returned the first, visible only when several widgets are focused at once, and `ConcatenatedLineBuffer::getLine()` ends with an unreachable `throw`.

